---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import Cta from "../components/Cta.astro";
import PageHead from "../components/PageHead.astro";
import {
  AUDIENCES,
  CHECKED,
  MOVES,
  NOT_FOR_YOU,
  SESSIONBOARD_URL,
  SESSIONIZE_URL,
  SPEAKER_CHANGES,
  STRUCTURAL,
  WHERE_THEY_WIN,
} from "../data/comparison";

/*
 * This page stopped being a comparison and became an estimate.
 *
 * The reader is not deciding whether to leave. They are pricing the move, in
 * the week the call opens, and the thing that stalls a switch is never the
 * feature list — it is the four boxes nobody can tell them about: what comes
 * out, what goes in, what has no equivalent, and what a speaker notices. So
 * `MOVES` leads and the product-versus-product material sits under it, for the
 * colleague who will ask "why not just use Sessionize?" in the next meeting.
 *
 * The rule this page is written under: nothing here characterises a
 * competitor's product as bad, every row they win is on the page, and every
 * claim about somebody else's product is dated and linked. A comparison a
 * reader can catch being unfair costs more than the deal it was written to win.
 *
 * Sessionize's 499 USD is on the page because they publish it and we link to
 * it. Sessionboard's monthly figure is not, and deliberately: their page states
 * one, but it renders identically against all three plans when read back, so
 * the number we would print is one we could not stand behind. The row says what
 * is not in doubt — three plans, a published price, a sales conversation for
 * the top one — and links out. Our own bill is on /pricing, itemised, and the
 * subtraction is the reader's to do.
 */
---

<Base
  title="Switching"
  description="What it costs to move a conference programme onto Podium: what a CSV brings across, what has no equivalent here, what your speakers notice, and where Sessionize and Sessionboard are the better buy."
>
  <PageHead
    eyebrow="Switching"
    title="What a switch actually costs you."
    lede={`Four things decide whether this is survivable: what comes out of the tool you are leaving, what goes into this one, what has no equivalent here, and what your speakers notice. Claims about anyone else's product were checked against their own pages in ${CHECKED}.`}
  />

  <section>
    <div class="wrap">
      <span class="eyebrow">What moves</span>
      <h2>Two things import cleanly. Four do not move at all.</h2>
      <p class="sub">
        A CSV creates people and the event roster. For the rest the honest answer is that last year
        stays where it is.
      </p>
      {/* `.stack` turns this into a list of records below 40rem. The third
          column is the whole point of the table and it is the one a phone
          hides behind a sideways scroll. */}
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table class="matrix stack">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">What you are moving</th>
              <th scope="col">How</th>
              <th scope="col">What that means in practice</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {
              MOVES.map((m) => (
                <tr>
                  <th scope="row">{m.item}</th>
                  <td class="mine" data-label="How">{m.how}</td>
                  <td data-label="What that means">{m.note}</td>
                </tr>
              ))
            }
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <div class="notice" style="margin-top: 1.75rem">
        <p>
          <strong>Nobody has publicly moved a live conference onto Podium yet.</strong> The import
          runs against a real database on every commit, and it has never been pointed at an export
          from a commercial tool in anger. Budget an afternoon for the file, not five minutes.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band">
    <div class="wrap">
      <span class="eyebrow">What your speakers notice</span>
      <h2>They did not choose any of this.</h2>
      <p class="sub">
        Four things change for the two hundred people who never see an admin screen. One of them is
        a real loss.
      </p>
      <div class="cards two">
        {
          SPEAKER_CHANGES.map((s) => (
            <div class="card reveal">
              <h3>{s.title}</h3>
              <p>{s.body}</p>
            </div>
          ))
        }
      </div>
      <p class="nextstep"><a href="/features">What a speaker sees once they are in →</a></p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="wrap">
      <span class="eyebrow">Who each is for</span>
      <h2>Start here and you may not need the rest.</h2>
      <div class="cards">
        {
          AUDIENCES.map((a) => (
            <div class="card reveal">
              <h3>{a.name}</h3>
              <p><strong>{a.built_for}</strong></p>
              <p>{a.body}</p>
              {a.href && (
                <p class="jump">
                  <a href={a.href} rel="nofollow noopener">Their pricing page →</a>
                </p>
              )}
            </div>
          ))
        }
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band">
    <div class="wrap">
      <span class="eyebrow">The structural difference</span>
      <h2>Everything below holds whatever ships next.</h2>
      <p class="sub">
        There is no feature matrix here. It would mean asserting from the outside what two
        proprietary products do, and it would be stale the day either ships a release. These rows
        are licensing, hosting and published packaging.
      </p>
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table class="matrix">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col"></th>
              <th scope="col" class="mine">Podium</th>
              <th scope="col">Sessionize</th>
              <th scope="col">Sessionboard</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {
              STRUCTURAL.map((r) => (
                <tr>
                  <th scope="row">{r.row}</th>
                  <td class="mine">{r.podium}</td>
                  <td>{r.sessionize}</td>
                  <td>{r.sessionboard}</td>
                </tr>
              ))
            }
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      {/* The explicit {" "} are load-bearing. A newline before an element at the
          start of a line is stripped in the build, which ran these three
          together as "decide:Sessionize andSessionboard". */}
      <p class="aside">
        Pricing and packaging change. Confirm both on their own pages before you decide:{" "}
        <a href={SESSIONIZE_URL} rel="nofollow noopener">Sessionize</a>{" "}and{" "}
        <a href={SESSIONBOARD_URL} rel="nofollow noopener">Sessionboard</a>. Do not take a
        competitor's word for it, ours included.
      </p>
      <p class="nextstep"><a href="/pricing">Our side of that table, itemised →</a></p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="wrap">
      <span class="eyebrow">Where they win</span>
      <h2>Five things the incumbents do better, today.</h2>
      <p class="sub">None of these is fixable by writing a better sentence about it.</p>
      <div class="cards">
        {
          WHERE_THEY_WIN.map((w) => (
            <div class="card reveal">
              <h3>{w.title}</h3>
              <p>{w.body}</p>
            </div>
          ))
        }
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band-dark">
    <div class="wrap">
      <span class="eyebrow">Be honest with yourself</span>
      <h2>Do not pick Podium if any of these is true.</h2>
      <p class="sub">
        Better to find this out here than in February. If two or more land, buy something hosted
        and enjoy your conference.
      </p>
      <ul class="crosses">{NOT_FOR_YOU.map((n) => <li>{n}</li>)}</ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <Cta
    heading="Still here? Go and use it."
    sub="Fifteen minutes in a conference already mid-flight will tell you more than this page did."
    secondaryHref="/features"
    secondaryLabel="See the screens first"
  />
</Base>
